Z||000000||Alex2004||12-09-2001||12:56 AM||||Today i took my wheel and caliper off to fix the loose pad, grease retaining bolts etx....well i also have bought new pads, so i was planning on just putting the new ones on.  Well my caliper piston refuses to move, we took the cap of the m/c, we even broke lose the bleeder but still nothing... i managed to get the old pads which do not need to be replaced back on.  this was on the left rear wheel.  We rebuilt the other caliper about 3 months ago. Do you think i should just replace the caliper with a new one from autozone since they are only $33 w/ a core?  I also notice kinda a grease or dried fluid on the piston itself couldn't tell where it was coming from exactly... is that normal?  Let me know what you think!  Thanks!<P>Alex..||||reg||4
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||12-09-2001||03:42 AM||||Hey Alex!<BR>As far as the rear caliper goes, the piston is rotated to get it to retract...<BR>If you need the tool, get it at NAPA or other parts store...<BR>If you still can't get it to retract, replace BOTH calipers...<BR>And if you replace the calipers, replace the flex lines at the same time...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
